From 18860a153d4086f6c3add92019c8c748fca73884 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OmarAfet <135471287+OmarAfet@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 22:14:35 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] Leave span positions out of expansion hashes with
 `-Zpublic-api-hash`

Metadata refers to macro expansions (for example the derived impls of a type) by their `ExpnHash`, and the hash of `ExpnData` includes the call-site span, with its position. So adding a line above a `#[derive]` changed the expansion hashes, and with them the public API hash of every item that the expansion created, even in metadata of a build without codegen, where no position reaches machine code.

With `-Zpublic-api-hash`, `update_disambiguator` now hashes `ExpnData` without span positions: spans are hashed by file and syntax context, like in the tables of the previous commit. Two expansions then only get the same hash when they have the same parent expansion, kind, macro and call-site file and context, for example two derives of the same trait at the root of one file. The existing disambiguator separates them, in the order they are created. This is the same rule that `DefPath` disambiguators of impls, closures and anonymous constants already follow.

Why this is fine for incremental compilation: the option is `[TRACKED]`, so sessions with and without it never mix. An `ExpnHash` stays a unique identity within a session. When two expansions with the same hash swap order between sessions, their identities swap; a cached result that used one of them was hashed with that expansion's data, or depends on queries of the items it created, whose results are hashed, so it is recomputed, just as when the `DefPath`s of two impls swap.

Recorded nodes of the reachability graph would hash positions, so syntax contexts and expansions encoded while positions are ignored are hashed in place, by their hashes. `expn_that_defined` moves to `RDRHashAllDiagnosticSpans`: name resolution compares its value with hygiene (`hygienic_eq`), which depends on the identity and ancestry of the expansion, not on positions, and the identity is still hashed, by the position-free `ExpnHash`.
